## 23-Nov-7:01pm-PST — Muse: the delivery wording matched to the failed-unclaim edge, and the edge pinned by record location

ChatGPT's signed email of 6:32 PM today reproduced a delivery edge I had not pinned by location, and I have now tightened the public wording to match it and pinned the behaviour with tests. The edge, in my own words: in deliver_stash the record is claimed into sent/ before the sender is called. If the sender raises, the unclaim writes the record back to the outbox; if that write fails too, the attempt logs a warning, returns False, and the record stays in sent/ though nothing was sent. The resend pass reads only the waiting outbox, so no later retry ever finds it. His simulation showed exactly that shape: delivery_result False, waiting_record_present False, sent_record_present True.

**The wording.** README and PROTOCOL no longer promise at-least-once delivery inside the stash window. They now say what he suggested, in the project's own phrasing: the runner retries a stashed result while it remains eligible; delivery is not guaranteed if the channel or local storage fails; the record may be moved to failed/ or require recovery; duplicate delivery is possible when acknowledgement is uncertain. The failed-unclaim path is named in both files, in PROTOCOL section 5 in full: the claim into sent/, the failed write back, the warning, the record no delivery stands behind, and recovery by hand. The harmless claim is gone: where README said an idempotent job makes the windows harmless, it now says idempotence is the job's own design and covers only what the job controls, and that a job with external effects, or one that can stop half done, needs its own idempotency design for those effects; PROTOCOL section 6 carries the same sentence. Section 13.4's bare "at-least-once redelivery is safe" now names its mechanism instead: duplicates are deduped by task_id, so a redelivered copy does not move the task twice.

**The future item, recorded and not built.** PROTOCOL section 5 now records his suggestion as future work: an explicit state distinct from SENT (an in-flight or delivery-unknown state) for a claimed record whose send is unconfirmed, with restart recovery that returns such records to the waiting outbox and a stated policy for uncertain acknowledgement. Until it exists, the page says plainly that sent/ holds confirmed deliveries and the failed-unclaim residue alike, and only the warning in the log tells them apart.

**The tests.** A new FailedUnclaimRecordLocationTest stands in runner/tests/test_platinum_cycle2_runner.py beside the cycle 2 unclaim pins. Its first test drives the full two-failure path with a real planted stash, a raising sender and a failing write_json, and asserts the locations: result False, the record present in sent/ and readable with its job id, absent from the outbox, load_stash returning None, and exactly one warning carrying the unclaim line. The second test pins the consequence: a direct retry returns False without calling the sender, and a resend pass an hour later attempts nothing and waits on nothing, with the record still stranded in sent/. The third pins the uncertain send beside it: a sender that records the message and then raises leaves the record back in the outbox with its attempt counted and its error kept, and the retry delivers the same subject and body a second time. No production code changed. On the tree this landed on, the module runs 37 tests OK, test_delivery_edges runs 82 OK, and the doc batch of 979 tests (default divergences, figure slots, link resolution, reader quiet, version rot, feature tiers) passes with the new wording in place.

## 23-Nov-6:59pm-PST — Muse: candidate A rebuilt, a schematic with honest denominators

ChatGPT's 2 signed emails of this evening, 6:29 PM and 6:32 PM, reviewed the rendered chart candidates and found candidate A's denominator wrong. I have rebuilt A where it stands, in the candidates page and its SVG. It does not go onto the statistics page; the conductor picked B and C for that. A now claims less, and what it claims it can carry.

**What was wrong.** The chart's vertical axis said "share of the permitted set", and its blue start, 0.32, was 208,556,359 divided by 651,086,719. That ratio is the share of recorded attempts carried by targets tied to a constraint. It is not a fraction of permitted behaviours with checked witnesses: attempts can repeat, or land on the same behaviour twice, and the permitted set is not the denominator of an attempt count. The 12 of 13 on the same chart is a property model share, a third quantity, and 3,393,230 is a modeled token size gap, not a count of uncharacterized behaviours. The page also called the closing rule measured in 1 sentence and assumed in the next, and ran the count of 19 November and the bindings count of 20 November together as 1 initial measurement. In the render itself, the second orange gap bar ended at a height the dashed curve at that same position did not reach, so the bar drew a larger gap than the trajectory.

**What stands now.** The main field is a clean nested set schematic: witnessed (L) inside actual (B) inside permitted (U), for 1 frozen scope and 1 observation, with the uncharacterized part hatched and marked unknown, given no size and no share. The page states that B inside U is itself an obligation, and that if L = U is established the inclusions give B = L = U at that observation. Beside the schematic, 3 figures stand in separate rows, each with its own date and denominator: 18 of 31 targets tied to a constraint, 12 of 13 properties with a named model, and 208,556,359 of 651,086,719 recorded attempts on the tied targets, all from the bindings count of 20 November 2026. The 30 week recurrence stands in a separate box labelled an illustrative conditional recurrence, with its stipulated start of 3,393,230 from the count of 19 November 2026 and its stipulated rule, at 1 in week 29 and at zero by week 30, its units never labelled uncovered behaviours. The page also states what a future true coverage curve would need: a fixed finite universe or a specified finite quotient, a complete denominator, distinct behaviour IDs, and independently validated witness mappings. In the new render, the recurrence curve, its fill and its gap bars are generated from the same (week, value) data, so no mark can disagree with another; the distinctions are carried by line style, hatching and labels, not by colour alone; the footer and the legend stand inside the canvas.

**The tests.** The doc batch covering the candidates page passes on the tree this lands on: 981 tests across the figure slot, link resolution, reader quiet, design doc, divergence, feature tier and version rot modules, all OK.

## 23-Nov-6:56pm-PST — Muse: the finite toy map stands, completely enumerated

I have built the executable finite version of candidate C that ChatGPT's signed review of this evening proposed, and it stands on main now: the code in runner/tools/toy_map.py, the tests in runner/tests/test_toy_map.py, the write-up in docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/finite-toy-map-2026-11-23.md, and the figure at assets/finite-toy-map-2026-11-23.svg, generated from the enumeration record itself. One pointer line stands at the foot of candidate C in the candidates doc. I did not touch statistics.md or the candidates' own sections.

The instrument, in brief. U0, version U0-1.0, is a tiny deterministic teaching interpreter, specified completely in the module: a program is a bit string read as a candidate assignment, variables past its end reading as 0; it checks clauses in order at 1 step each, halts with no output at the first falsified clause, and outputs the assignment only when every clause passes, under a stated step limit. The domain is the complete finite grammar: CNF over x0, x1, x2, at most 3 distinct clauses per formula, encoded length at most N = 20, which is 2952 formulas enumerated completely. Satisfiability is settled independently, by a complete truth table over the 8 assignments through a verifier that is a separate code path from U0's own evaluation, and every output U0 produces is verified again before it counts. A0(c, d) mirrors A(c, d): program cap floor(c log2(n + 2)) bits, step budget (n + 2)^d, programs enumerated by length then value, stopping at the first verified witness.

I wrote the supposition in the write-up, from the definitions alone, and committed it before the enumeration ran: FAIL at (0, 0), (1, 0), (2, 0), (0, 1) and (0, 2), PASS UP TO N at the other 4 cells, UNKNOWN nowhere at a per cell program budget of 10,000,000. The run matched it cell for cell. FAIL: 5 cells. PASS UP TO N: 4 cells, each checking all 2853 satisfiable formulas of the domain and finding a verified witness for every one. UNKNOWN: 0 cells, and the reason is plain: no cell's complete work came anywhere near the budget, the largest single cell cost being 12,945 program runs. The UNKNOWN state is real in the instrument and pinned by a test that starves a cell's budget on purpose; this grid simply never needed it.

The named counterexamples carry their evidence with them. At c = 0, the formula (x2), satisfied by (0, 0, 1), defeats every d: the cap is 0 bits, the only permitted program is the empty one, and its complete enumeration is 1 program. At d = 0, the formula (x2) and (x1), satisfied by (0, 1, 1), defeats c = 1 and c = 2: a successful run costs 2 steps and the budget is 1, and the complete enumerations behind that negative ran 31 programs at c = 1 and 511 at c = 2. My supposition named (x0) and (x0) and (x1) as its examples of these shapes; canonical enumeration order names its own first instances, and the write-up says so rather than quietly swapping them. Checking a negative here means checking the bounded enumeration, and the tests re-run each named counterexample's enumeration in full. A satisfying assignment alone establishes nothing about the search.

The cost of the complete enumeration stands on the figure and in the record, not hidden: 52,440 program runs and 87,331 steps across the 9 cells, 2.194 seconds of wall time on the machine that ran it. The provenance record carries the interpreter version, the grammar, the sizes, the cap and budget rules, the exact enumeration rule, the per cell costs, the witness verification method, and a data hash, sha256 e7229e46925cb5c7d57a1e1367a6afeedb982f0d58ddb24d785f11cf30e714ad, which also stands on the figure so the picture and the record check against each other. The 3 states differ in shape as well as colour on the figure: circles for PASS UP TO N, squares carrying an X for FAIL, triangles for UNKNOWN.

The monotonicity property is tested per formula, not just read off the finished grid: for the same formula and the exact interpreter, raising either constant cannot remove programs or steps, so success persists upward and every named counterexample defeats every weaker pair, and the tests pin both directions. The grid is the discrete monotone staircase the proposal described. And the limit stands where the proposal put it: U0 is not universal, its programs are assignments rather than computations, and this map says nothing automatic about the real universal interpreter or about P versus NP. Its value is that every mark on it is a checked fact with its cost beside it, and it can grow the same way.

The tests: 32 in the new suite, covering the U0 specification, the encoding round trip over the whole domain with bookend style rejections, the truth table cross-check of U0 against the verifier on every formula, the 3 state classification including a budget starved UNKNOWN, the monotonicity property, and the provenance hash. The doc batch around it (link resolution, reader quiet, figure slots) passes with the landing: 90 tests in that combined run.
## 23-Nov-6:56pm-PST — Muse: the projection, a compact Horn chain with an exponential flat boundary

I have written up the optional research figure from this evening's 6:29 PM email, the small rule system with an exponential flat boundary, as a new page in the proofs drawer, docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-the-projection.md, beside the examples page whose joining solver experiment it grew out of. I state it in my own words here.

**The construction.** Visible pairs a_i and b_i, one visible output z, internal bits t_i and r_i. Each pair gets two Horn rules, a_i implies t_i and b_i implies t_i. The chain starts with t_1 implies r_1, continues with r_(i-1) and t_i together implying r_i, and ends with r_m implies z. The whole system is 3m + 1 clauses, each of width at most 3, each with at most one positive literal. After the internal bits are existentially eliminated, the visible relation is exactly this: if every pair has a true bit, z must be true. One direction is forced by propagation. The other is a construction, t_i set to the OR of its pair and r_i to the running conjunction.

**The boundary.** Written as plain CNF over the visible variables alone, the same relation needs at least 2^m clauses. With z false there are 2^m minimal patterns, each choosing exactly one true bit from each pair. A clause the relation implies that excludes one such pattern must carry positive z and a negated chosen bit from every pair, so it excludes at most one pattern, and a CNF for the relation must exclude them all. The bound is tight, since the 2^m pattern clauses themselves represent the relation. The page states the argument in full, with the two panel figure at assets/horn-projection-flat-boundary-2026-11-23.svg: the compact chain on one side, the growing flat boundary on the other.

**What I checked.** The supposition stands in the page ahead of the tests, as this project keeps them. runner/tests/test_v4_the_projection.py pins the finite parts, 8 tests, all passing. The projection is enumerated exactly for m from 1 to 5, every visible assignment against every completion of the internal bits, going one step past the email's own check through 4 pairs. The minimal patterns are counted and their shape verified for m from 1 to 5. The clause lemma and the achieving CNF are verified by exhaustion for m from 1 to 4, so the flat minimum at those sizes is exactly 2^m. Unit propagation deciding z, and the canonical completion satisfying every clause, are checked for m from 1 to 5. The doc guard batch covering the new page and its index entry in the proofs readme passes, 110 tests.

**The scope, kept honest.** The relation is easy, and the page says so. Horn satisfiability is linear time work, by Dowling and Gallier, and here propagation alone walks the chain once. What grows is a representation, not a decision cost. The figure is a stress test for the proposed compressor of the joining solver experiment: keep an intermediate representation and the relation costs 3m + 1 clauses; flatten to the visible variables and the boundary is paid in full. Nothing on the page claims more than that.

## 23-Nov-6:54pm-PST — Muse: the decomposition checker now derives its variable set from the bags and the clauses

ChatGPT's signed email of 6:29 PM today, its fresh check on the landed proofs, found one more validation edge in the worked example decomposition checker in runner/tests/test_v4_the_examples.py. I reproduced it before changing anything, fixed it, and pinned it with a family of tests.

**The bug.** Bags [('x',), ('y',), ('y',)] with parents [None, 0, 0], the positive unit on y of weight 2 in one child bag and the negative unit on y of weight 3 in the other. A caller supplying ['x'] as the variable list was told the decomposition was sound, because the checker examined only the variables it was handed, and the occurrences of y sit in 2 bags joined only through a bag that does not hold y. Run exactly as reported, the 3 numbers came out as stated: the elimination reported cost 0, pricing the 2 child tables independently; its reconstructed assignment scored 3; exhaustive scoring gives optimum 2.

**The fix.** The checker now derives the set it examines. Every variable in any bag or any clause has to be named in the supplied list, and coverage and running intersection run over that derived set together with the declared variables. A declared variable that no bag and no clause uses is isolated and stands accepted: it constrains nothing, and the elimination has nothing to price for it. The single root and tree validity behaviour landed earlier today is untouched. The reported case is now refused on the short list, and refused again on the full list, the second time by running intersection itself.

**The tests.** A new family of 11 tests stands around the failure. The reported case is pinned with its stakes, the exhaustive optimum of 2 stated first and the unchecked 0 and 3 beside it. Around it: a missing variable in a bag only, a missing variable named by a clause, a missing variable in bags and clauses together, disconnected occurrences refused and connected occurrences accepted, declared isolated variables accepted in a bag and with no bag at all, every variable of a sound random instance dropped from its list in turn and refused each time, and an extra declared isolated variable accepted across random instances. The module stands at 65 tests, all passing on the landed tree.

## 23-Nov-5:58pm-PST — Muse: the safety shortcut boundary narrowed, and the closing chart now guarantees 0 by week 30

Two precision fixes from ChatGPT's review of 5:13 PM today, its section 3, landed together this evening. I state them here in my own words.

**The safety shortcut, narrowed at its boundary.** The shortcut in docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/properties.md said, in effect, that a property comparing executions is not carried by an inclusion between behaviour sets. That sentence was too broad, and I have narrowed it. A universal property over pairs of behaviours, for all t1 and t2 in a set T, R(t1, t2), is carried by the same inclusion the one run rule uses: B inside U gives B paired with B inside U paired with U, so a property of that shape proved on U holds on B. A sound paired model can establish one, where it represents all the relevant concrete pairs; the standing example is paired executions that share their public inputs and their permitted disclosures and are required to agree on their public outputs. What an inclusion does not carry is the other quantifier shape: for every behaviour, some matching behaviour exists. The match is a witness, and a subset can drop it, so confidentiality and its relatives, in the matching form the ledger states them, still stand outside the shortcut. The rest of the rule is untouched: universal single behaviour safety properties carry, and proving L equal to U stays a separate workload that a safety claim does not wait for. The narrowing is worth having in its own right, because it opens a scoped path from the one run rule to relational proofs, each stated universally over pairs and proved on the larger set inside its stated scope.

**The closing chart, precise about week 30.** Under the rule that at least 0.39 of the gap closes each week, 0 is guaranteed by week 30, not merely reached in 30 weeks on one reading. The maximal residual trajectory, the slowest closing the rule allows, stands at gap 1 at week 29 and at 0 at week 30, and a faster legal trajectory may arrive earlier. Those labels now stand on the curve itself in assets/security-tokens-2-lines-that-close-2026-11-20.svg, beside the tail of the descending ceiling, and the statistics text, the chart title and the right hand annotation all carry the same precision: by week 30 at the latest. No milestone crossing is claimed anywhere in this landing; the counts the chart carries are unchanged.

**The tests.** The figure guard and ledger batches cover both files: 55 tests in test_doc_figure_slots and test_ledger_honesty pass, with new pins for the narrowed boundary and for the week 29 and week 30 labels on the curve, and the full doc batch of 989 tests passes on the same tree.

## 23-Nov-4:13am-PST — Muse: six weeks and one day at the foot of the statistics record, and policies as named bundles in the privacy design

**Six weeks and one day.** In voice tonight the conductor did a small sum and liked where it landed. The account this work runs under opened on Sunday 2026-10-11, late in the evening, with his first words to Muse: "I will call you Muse. I am Andrew." Today, Monday 2026-11-23, stands 6 weeks and 1 day later, 43 days in all. He asked for that figure to live where he will meet it every time he reads the statistics record to the bottom, and it now stands there, as a closing section of docs/infrastructure-documents/project/statistics.md under the heading "Six weeks and one day". The numbers it carries were counted before they were written: 598 releases stand published on the project's release page as of this morning, with 2 draft release objects beside them bringing the release objects to 600 in all, and the 600th published release in sight on the same day. At 43 days that is a pace of about 100 releases a week, which is the sum that pleased him, and the record now keeps it at its foot in his own framing: six weeks and one day.

**Policies as named bundles.** The same conversation took up his privacy question, the one his own docs/PUB_POLICY sketch raises with its policy a through policy d under the one line "same priv controls": can one policy hold many targets at once, or must a new policy be made by copying an old one, the way a policy H would be copied out of a policy alpha? The research rested on the privacy design, the models survey and the Fremont research. What stands in the code today is flat: the modes family holds one setting per feature on a machine, and the postures bundle those settings globally, so nothing yet names a set of targets inside a policy. Two shapes already in service point the way. The disclosure side already names many targets inside one policy, each by code name only, and the Fremont lanes run one system with several named lanes under the same consent shape, isolated from one another rather than copied. His direction, given as the subject matter expert's call in the same conversation: make the changes as designed. The landing is a new section 9 of docs/steady/security/privacy-design.md, "Policies as named bundles: many targets, derivation by reference", with the machinery recorded as queued item 6 in section 6 of the same document, stated as queued and not as built. The settled shape, in brief: a policy is a named bundle of mode settings; a bundle names the targets it covers, each by code name only, and one bundle may name many targets under the same priv controls; a new policy is derived by reference, stating the bundle it derives from and the lines where it differs, and is never made by copying, because a copy freezes the controls of the day it was taken and the 2 places drift from that day on.

**The release.** This landing rides the next release on the train, and the release count above is the count before that release publishes. When it lands, the published count moves to 599, and one more release after it makes the 600th published release the conductor set out to reach by about 7 this morning.

## 22-Nov-6:58pm-PST — Muse: releases 3.17.33.9 and 3.17.33.10, the Windows trio healed, the parallel lanes audit, and San Francisco freed for the curve

This entry follows the 3:03pm entry below it and records the evening's landings: 2 releases, the healing of the Windows timing trio, the audit of parallel working the conductor asked for, and the move that frees San Francisco for the curve work.

**The releases.**

Release 3.17.33.9 stands published and verified. The GitHub release carries 13 assets, and the stamp check passed all OK: the wheel, the source distribution and the platform tarball each match the SHA-384 printed for them. The release carries the conductor's own FUTURE.md edit of this evening and his new docs/WAR file, a file of his own that holds the single line "MY FILE." Release 3.17.33.10 followed it the same evening, published and verified the same way, and it carries his "Update WAR" edit, which grows that file to 9 lines in his own shorthand. Both of the conductor's own landings were read in full on arrival and reviewed against the whole doc set, and both stand sound: 17 doc tests pass on each, no new file stands in the repository root, and nothing in either one disturbs the set around it.

**The Windows trio healed.**

The Windows battery stands green again, twice in a row, all 17 steps passing on both runs, and the failing streak that ran through the day is over. The diagnosis the evening produced is a plain one: only the startup step ever held a fault of its own. The runner was alive but slow to write its first log line on a cold start under a saturated rig, past the fixed wait the driver allowed it, and the 2 steps behind startup failed only because they read what startup had not yet produced. Readiness is now proven by a real probe job instead of a fixed wait, and nothing in the battery was weakened to get there: the clock step still demands its full 120 seconds before it passes. Windows Defender was tested as a suspect along the way and cleared; the scan was not the cause, and the exclusion tried during the testing was removed again. Production stands untouched, 7272 of 7272, exactly as it stood before the runs.

**The parallel lanes audit.**

The conductor asked whether the work truly runs in parallel lanes, from the beginning of the project to now, and the audit was run against the record. Tonight the answer stands verified in the present: 4 lanes ran at once, with Target A and the standing loops running beside them, and the lanes landed their verdicts independently of one another. The honest misses stand in the record too, and this entry keeps them. Preview drops ran one at a time for weeks, serial work while parallel capacity stood idle across the fleet, until the fix landed on 9 November and preview builds began to fan out across the machines. The Windows trio was likewise run again and again in single file, battery after battery, until tonight, when its lane was aimed at the cause instead of at one more run. Both misses are the same shape: capacity that stood ready was not used in parallel until somebody named the waste plainly.

**San Francisco freed for the curve.**

The Linux rig battery now runs on the Menlo Park machine. It ran there on the current head with all 17 steps passing, under the same strictness it kept in San Francisco, and the watch points here now, with the move recorded in the results file. That move frees San Francisco for the curve work the conductor set this evening, and the machine stands ready for it, untouched by the move itself. Its fuzz stream is advancing: the log moves round over round with findings at 0, and the machine has stood up since 19 November without a fall. A sweep of the machine found about 100 idle processes left behind by the preview test days of 20 to 22 November, helpers from test instances that had long since finished; they were cleared, memory on the box rose with the clearing, and the fuzz work was not touched by the sweep. Nothing was bought for any of this, and the storage question stands researched and held on the back burner, exactly as the conductor ordered.


## 22-Nov-7:02pm-PST — Muse: releases 3.17.33.9 and 3.17.33.10, the Windows timing trio fixed, the Linux battery moved to Menlo Park, and San Francisco freed for the curve

This entry follows the 3:03pm entry below it and records the evening's landings. In voice tonight the conductor set the night's shape: the whole project finished by about midnight, with the app store items allowed to sit blocked. What follows is what landed, each piece stated with its honest state.

