For days the Germany rig battery failed the same 3 of its 17 steps, startup, build stamp and clock, while every confinement step passed in every run and the Linux battery passed on the same heads. The research the conductor called for today found that the 3 were never 3 faults. The battery allowed the runner a fixed 30 seconds to come up and then read its log for the starting line. On a cold start the runner is alive well inside that budget but its first log line lands after it, so startup read as failed; build stamp reads that same starting line, so it failed with it; and the clock step rides on the same start, so it failed too. 1 late start, 3 red steps. Windows Defender was the standing suspect for the delay, and it was tested and proven innocent: the start was no faster with the test tree excluded from its scanning, and the exclusion was removed again afterwards. The fix is in the battery driver: readiness is now proven by a real probe job, a signed job sent to the runner and its result read back, instead of a fixed wait for a log line. Nothing was weakened to get the pass: the clock step still demands its full timing window, and every step asserts what it asserted before. The battery has now passed in full twice in a row, all 17 steps each time, and production on the rig stands untouched, 7272 of 7272. The honest note: the fix stands tonight in the rig harness where the battery lives, and landing it on main is the remaining step on this item.

**The Linux battery moved to Menlo Park, and San Francisco freed.**

The Linux half of the rig battery now runs on the Menlo Park VM instead of the San Francisco rig. It ran there in full and passed all 17 steps, and the watch now points at Menlo Park, with a dated note recording the flip. 1 honest difference stands recorded with the result: this cell runs the job user step at audit rather than enforce, its bubblewrap limit is recorded beside the verdict, and the other 15 steps match the San Francisco runs exactly. San Francisco is therefore free for the work the conductor set for it: the curve bending fuzzing, the unbounded data growth he described on 16 November. The rig stands verified tonight: up since its boot on 19 November, its hybrid fuzz run advancing, at round 869,250 with 0 findings at the last check. About 100 idle processes left over from the preview test runs of the past days were swept off it tonight, freeing its memory for the fuzz work.

**The conductor's own landings.**

3 landings today are the conductor's own hand, each reviewed on landing and each sound. At 6:07pm an edit to docs/FUTURE.md, 1 line changed in the app store requirements bullet. At 6:08pm a new file, docs/WAR, his own notebook page, opening with the words MY FILE. At 6:26pm an update to that same page, growing it to 9 lines: the war project line, the privacy note, 4 legal lines and the word GO. Release 3.17.33.9 carries the first 2 of these and 3.17.33.10 carries the third. 1 note stands on the WAR update: a spelling slip in its first added line, homing-pigeaon for homing-pigeon, left exactly as he landed it, his file and his call.

