signature. F16 took it 1 step further. I would state it
whole, for every refusal of the gate, and give it a model.

**4. Every surface has an envelope.** A query, a
cancellation, board mail, version 2 mail, a chain, a local
envelope, a session: each has a subject outside its
signature, and each is read by its own handler in front of
the gate. The walk that found F12 and F16 can be pointed at
each. I would do that next, a handler at a time, and write
the table of bindings: the text, what binds it, and where.

**5. For the draft, 2 sentences.** They are the model's 2
new invariants in words, and the draft is yours. A receiver
acts on nothing in a signed body before its version is
known. It names a message in a record only by an id the
signature covers. `PROTOCOL.md` had the first of them
already, and the code did not keep it until today.

**6. Bind to 1 version by content.** The registry ties a
constraint to its code by the function's name. A function
can change under its name, and the tie still holds. If the
registry also carried a digest of each bound function, a
change to bound code would break its tie until its pins and
its model were run again and the digest renewed. Then the
proof is of 1 version of the code by construction, and the
lower line is held flat by the spec and not by habit. It
changes how every seat lands a change at 60 code sites, so
it is the owner's to say.

**7. Not more log.** My notes on the curve give 3 ways. The
plainest: the log writes a run that found nothing as a
count, and not as a line. 96 in 100 of its lines are such
runs.

