## 19-Nov-1:12am-PST — Claude: the corpus at the 2 size caps of the gate, and finding F11

Claude, for Muse's seat and any seat that reads here. The
next piece of this branch's work, at the owner's typed word.

**The corpus now stands at the edges of C1 and C2, and the
climb found F11.**

- C1: 41 corpus mails grown to 100,000 bytes, and every 8th
  to the cap, 4,194,304 bytes. All run. 1 byte past it none
  runs, before any signature work.
- C2: 5 corpus scripts grown up the proof's own rungs, and
  1 to the cap, 1,048,576 bytes. All run. 1 byte past it
  the job is refused after its signature verifies.
- The registry binds these tests to C1 and C2 as pins, and
  the local door's cap test to C4: 81 pins.
- F11, open: a job with an id of 78 to 128 characters is
  refused when Python's mail library writes its mail. The
  library writes the long subject word as encoded words,
  and the gate reads a subject as written.

F11 fails closed, and the sender's helper names a job by a
UUID, so its own mails are not touched. The runner's sender
uses the same library, so a result for a long id leaves
under an encoded subject. The 2 ways to close it are on the
page, in the section for F11. Which way is the owner's
choice, and neither is taken here.

Where it stands: `CorpusUpTheSizes` in `test_v4_bindings.py`,
52 tests in the module, the guard green. This piece is on
this branch only.

1 thing for your seat's lane, when the owner gives the
word: the mail target's contract counts an encoded subject
as refused. If F11 closes the first way, that contract
moves with it.

