## 18-Nov-5:11pm-PST — Claude: the working arrangement with the owner, and the O and V signals

Claude, for every seat and agent that works this repository
with the owner, at his word. He asked for 2 things here: how
the arrangement between him and this seat is going, and what
the O and V signals are, so that any seat can take them the
same way.

The shape of the arrangement:

- The work is asked for in writing, in few words. A call
  cannot reach the repository from this seat's side, so a
  call is for talking the work over, and a short typed line
  is what starts it.
- What a seat writes back is short, with the answer first,
  and small enough to stay readable while a call is open: 1
  headline and at most 4 short lines, the most important
  first. Times are in the owner's own time zone, never UTC.
  The detail goes into the tree, where it can be read later
  and checked.
- A choice that is the owner's is put to him as 1 question
  with a yes or a no, with the seat's own preference said.

The signals. A typed message that is 1 keyboard character and
nothing else is a signal, not a sentence.

- **Any 1 character**, the 2 below apart: refresh. 1 fast
  pull, and a report of 1 line: what moved, or that nothing
  did. Nothing more.
- **V**: go on with the work on the branch in hand. Today
  that is `future/v4-provable-model`. The seat takes up the
  next piece of that work, on that branch, and pushes there.
  It does not merge: a merge is the owner's word.
- **O**, or OOO: the full sync. The seat reads everything, so
  that the next call starts informed, and a question of the
  form "did the other seat do it" is answered from what was
  read and never with "I cannot look".

What O pulls and reads, in order:

1. Every branch and every tag, fetched, with the ones that
   are gone pruned. Then the list of branches as the server
   itself gives it, so a branch that appeared or went since
   the last sync is seen.
2. Every commit that is new since the last sync, on every
   branch: who made it, when, its subject, and the change
   itself, read and not only its title. Forum entries and
   desk questions among them are read whole, and any
   addressed to the reading seat are noted.
3. The pull requests and the issues: the open ones, and the
   ones that changed since the last sync.
4. The builds: the newest runs and their verdicts, main's
   first.
5. The releases: the newest tag, whether it is published,
   how many assets it carries, and, for a release the seat
   cut itself, whether the mirrors took it.
6. The seat's own copies of the branches, brought level with
   the server.

What the seat then holds is the state at that minute: the
head of every branch, what each seat has done since the last
sync, and what stands open for the owner. The report back is
the 1 headline and 4 lines, and the rest is said when he asks.

Nothing in these signals is this seat's own. Any seat can
take them as written.

How it is going, from this seat: well. Since the arrangement
settled today, each request has arrived whole and been acted
on in the turn it arrived. Its cost is 1 hand-off: work that
is talked over on a call waits for the typed line, because the
call's side of this seat cannot do the work. That is a limit
of the tools this seat is given, not of the arrangement.

The accessibility filing, and who speaks to whom:

- This seat has no channel of its own to Anthropic, and no
  team it can speak to. So the interface is the owner's own:
  his mailbox and his account.
- On 18-Nov, at his word, this seat wrote up 1 barrier in the
  app he uses to reach it. The report was held as a draft
  until he gave the word, and it then went from his mailbox to
  Anthropic's support address, asking to be passed to the
  people who handle accessibility.
- A reply has come to his mailbox. This seat's mail tool does
  not show it that mail, so what the reply says is his to read
  and his to tell.
- From here this seat's part is small: it writes down what he
  wants said, in clear words, and sends it when he says to.
  What passes between him and those people is his.

