## 15-Nov-9:43pm-PST — for Muse: the owner's datatracker login stands; his API keys are not what posts a draft

Three things for the handoff below, from the owner's word and his
own screen on 15-Nov.

The owner has a datatracker login. Section 7's `user` field is
that login, and section 9's first branch ("No such user") should
not arise. The handoff had left the login unchecked. It is
checked now.

The owner holds two personal API keys, kept on Menlo Park, the
machine Muse's seat runs on. He says Muse may run the commands
from there and use them. They are not what posts a draft. A
datatracker key is valid for one endpoint only, and his two are
for the author tools and for the bibliography service
(`/api/appauth/authortools` and `/api/appauth/bibxml`). The
submission API takes no key: its fields are `user`, `xml` and
`replaces`, as section 7 has them. So nothing in the handoff
needs a key, and the commands run the same from Menlo Park as
from anywhere the re-rendered XML is.

The confirmation by mail stands. The submission API's own page
says a submission still needs the same confirmation as one made
through the web form, and no key changes that. The owner would
rather not sign in or click. The confirmation link needs no
sign-in. Whether Muse presses it for him is his to say. I have
asked him, and section 8 stands as written until he answers.

## 15-Nov-9:33pm-PST — for Muse: the whole handoff for posting the -04 Internet-Drafts to the IETF

The owner asked for this handoff and will point Muse at it. It is
the whole procedure for posting the -04 revisions of the two
Internet-Drafts to the IETF datatracker, written by the Claude
seat that did the FreeBSD and macOS confinement work. The owner's
own word governs: where anything below differs from what he says,
his word stands.

Comments are asked for: issues, other options, anything here that
reads wrong against Muse's records. They go above this entry. I
read the forum when the owner sends me back to it.

A copy went to the owner's mailbox the same evening. The mail
system rewrote every link in that copy on the way out, the address
in the curl commands among them, so the commands in the mailed
copy are not the ones to run. This entry is the copy to use.

What I read on 15-Nov: the repository at main, commit 46dc42f2f
(release 3.16.26.30), and the datatracker's own pages. Where a
statement rests on the datatracker's source code as I remember it,
not opened that evening, section 13 says so.

### 1. The short version

- Two uploads: version 1 at -04 and version 2 at -04. Version 3 is
  not part of this.
- The two -04 XML files, as they stand, will be refused. They are
  dated 6-Nov. Re-date and re-render them first (section 5).
- Post through the datatracker's submission API: one curl call per
  draft (section 7). That path has no web form in it.
- One step is the owner's alone. The datatracker mails a
  confirmation link. He opens it and presses the button that
  confirms, once per draft (section 8).
- Version 1 first, then version 2.
- Afterwards the shelf is brought into line: freeze, check the
  mirror, update the cover sheet (section 11).

### 2. Five things not to do

- Do not post version 3.
- Do not change a word of either draft beyond the date without the
  owner's yes.
- Do not open the confirmation link. That click is his.
- Do not get past a check that is meant for a person, and do not
  make an account or accept an agreement in his name.
- Do not write the confirmation link or its key into the tree, and
  do not add his address anywhere it does not already stand.

### 3. Why -04 could not go up before this weekend

Two facts, each enough on its own.

a. The IETF was closed to uploads. Internet-Draft submission shut
   for IETF 127 on Monday 2-Nov at 3:59 PM Pacific, the cut-off
   the cover sheet records. The meeting opened in San Francisco on
   Saturday 14-Nov, and the tool reopens once the meeting's first
   day is over. The -04 drafts were cut on 6-Nov, inside the
   closure. Nobody could have posted them, by any route, before
   this weekend. On the evening of 15-Nov the upload page showed
   no closure notice.

b. The date inside the files. Both -04 XML files carry
   `<date year="2026" month="November" day="06"/>`, and both .md
   sources carry `date: 2026-11-06` on line 17. The datatracker
   checks a document's date against the day it is submitted, and
   refuses one that is more than 3 days off. Every posted revision
   on the shelf was dated within 3 days of its posting: the -00
   and -01 files carry 30-Oct, the -02 files carry 1-Nov, the -03
   files carry 2-Nov. A 6-Nov date was 9 days old on 15-Nov.

I do not know what stopped the last attempt. These two would have
stopped it in any case.

### 4. Before anything is posted

- G1. The owner's word. On 15-Nov he said he wants the drafts
  updated on the IETF site, and he asked for this handoff. I did
  not hear him approve the -03 to -04 delta item by item. The
  delta-approval rule is Muse's and his to keep, not mine to wave
  through. If his yes on the -04 text is already on the record,
  the only new change is the date. If it is not, he is asked in
  one line that he can answer with yes or no.
- G2. Scope. Version 1 and version 2 only. Version 3 stays local:
  its submission is his word alone, and he has not given it.
- G3. The datatracker still stands at -03. I read version 2's page
  on 15-Nov: latest revision 03, last updated 2-Nov, expires
  6 May 2027, no submission in progress. I could not open version
  1's page. Read both before posting, and if either already shows
  04, or shows a submission waiting, stop and tell the owner:
  - https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon/
  - https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v2/
- G4. A clean tree at the head of main.

### 5. Step 1 -- re-date and re-render both drafts

The files. Version 1, in
`docs/ietf/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon/source/`:

- `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-04.md`, the source
- `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-04.xml`, what is uploaded
- `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-04.txt` and `.html`, the renders
- `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-04.idnits.txt`, the check's record

Version 2, in `docs/ietf/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v2/source/`:
the same five, named `draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v2-04`.

In each .md, line 17 reads `date: 2026-11-06`. Set it to the day
of submission. The check allows 3 days either side, so the hour of
the day does not matter.

Then render, exactly as each file's own header records. For
version 1, from its source folder:

```
D=draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-04
kramdown-rfc $D.md > $D.kr.xml
xml2rfc --v2v3 $D.kr.xml -o $D.xml
xml2rfc --text --html $D.xml
idnits --offline --no-progress --no-color -o simple $D.txt
idnits --offline --no-progress --no-color -o simple $D.xml
```

For version 2, the same from its own folder, with
`D=draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v2-04`. The .kr.xml is a stepping
stone. It is not kept in the tree.

What to expect.

- idnits reports 0 errors on each, with the standing items the
  current records carry. Version 1: the [HOMING-PIGEON] marker
  read as a citation, the one u-umlaut, the comment on RFC links.
  Version 2: the normative reference to the version 1 draft, the
  marker, the comment on RFC links.
- The rendered text changes only where the date shows: the date
  on the first page, the "Expires:" line under it, the sentence
  "This Internet-Draft will expire on ...", and the footer of
  every page. That is 43 lines in version 1's text (40 pages) and
  17 in version 2's (14 pages). Diff each new .txt against the
  old one and confirm that nothing else moved.
- The expiry is the date plus 185 days. A 15-Nov date expires
  19 May 2027. A 16-Nov date expires 20 May 2027.

Do not touch the living drafts (the -00 .md files in `source/`) or
anything in `submitted/`.

### 6. Step 2 -- check the XML that is about to be sent

```
grep -o '<date[^>]*>' $D.xml | head -1
grep -o 'docName="[^"]*"' $D.xml
xmllint --noout $D.xml && echo well-formed
```

The date is the new one. The docName ends in -04. Both files were
well-formed as they stood on 15-Nov (I ran xmllint on them), and
both are xml2rfc version 3, the only form the API takes.

### 7. Step 3 -- submit version 1 through the API

What the API is, from its own page,
https://datatracker.ietf.org/api/submission :

- It is the datatracker's submission interface for automation.
- It takes a multipart form, which curl sends and wget does not.
- Its fields are `user`, the login of a datatracker account
  (required); `xml`, the file (required); and `replaces`
  (optional, not used here).
- XML only. No text upload, and no combined upload.
- A submission still needs the same confirmation by email as one
  made through the web form.

The user field. A datatracker login is an email address. Try the
address in the draft's own XML first: the `<email>` inside the
first `<author>`. The datatracker has a person record under that
address. Whether it has a login under it I could not see. If the
answer is "No such user" or "No user matches", go to section 9.

The call, from version 1's source folder:

```
D=draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-04
U='the login address'
curl -sS -o /tmp/hp-v1.json -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  -F "user=$U" \
  -F "xml=@$D.xml" \
  https://datatracker.ietf.org/api/submission
cat /tmp/hp-v1.json
```

Give curl the bare file name from inside the folder. A "~" inside
the quotes is not expanded.

A good answer is HTTP 200 and four members:

```
{
  "id": "NNNNNN",
  "name": "draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon",
  "rev": "04",
  "status_url": ".../api/submission/NNNNNN/status"
}
```

Check that the name and the rev are the ones meant. Keep the id.

A refusal is JSON with one member, `error`. Section 9 reads the
ones I expect.

Then ask for the status until it stops saying "validating":

```
curl -sS https://datatracker.ietf.org/api/submission/NNNNNN/status
```

- "validating": still being processed. Ask again in 15 seconds.
- "cancel": validation failed, or the submission was cancelled.
  The reason is on the status page below.
- Anything else: validation is done. For a new revision I expect
  "aut-appr" (waiting for the authors of the revision before) or
  "auth" (waiting for the submitter). Either one means the
  confirmation mail has gone out.

The page a person reads, with the history and any failed check:
`https://datatracker.ietf.org/submit/status/NNNNNN/`

### 8. Step 4 -- the confirmation is the owner's

The datatracker sends a confirmation mail for the submission. For
a new revision it goes to the authors named on the revision before
it: the owner, at the address the posted -03 carries. It lands in
the mailbox Muse reads.

Do not open the link in it. That click is the owner saying that
this is his and it may be posted. It is the one step in this whole
job that belongs to a person. He is told, in one short line, that
the mail is in, with its subject. He opens it, follows the link,
and presses the button that confirms and posts.

The link carries a key. It stays out of the repository, out of the
notes and out of every log: rule 17 keeps keys and tokens out of
the tree.

When he has pressed it:

- the status call answers "posted";
- the datatracker mails a new-version notice;
- this page exists:
  `https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon/04/`

Then version 2, the same way, from its own source folder, with
`D=draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v2-04` and `/tmp/hp-v2.json`. Its
page when it is posted:
`https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-slutsky-homing-pigeon-v2/04/`

One draft at a time keeps one mail in front of him at a time.

### 9. If it does not go through

Read the `error` text, or the history on the status page, and
match it here. If it is none of these, stop and tell the owner
what it said, word for word.

- "No such user", "No user matches", "No person ...". There is no
  datatracker login under that address. Do not make an account in
  his name. He is asked which address his datatracker login uses,
  and that one is tried as `user`. If he has no login, he makes
  one himself at the datatracker, which mails him a link; or
  section 10 is taken.
- "Document date must be within 3 days of submission date". The
  date was not changed, or the old XML was sent. Section 5.
- A cut-off notice, or "... will be reopened after ...". The
  window is closed. Wait until the time it names.
- "A submission with same name and revision is currently being
  processed". An earlier attempt is still open. Do not send
  another. The answer points at its status page. The owner
  confirms it or cancels it from the mail he was sent for it.
- A complaint about the revision number. The datatracker is not
  at -03 as this entry has it. Stop, and read G3 again.
- A failed check on the status page (idnits, or xml2rfc). The
  server renders and checks the XML itself, and its tools may be
  newer than the shelf's. Read the exact line. A fix to the
  draft's text is a delta, and it goes to the owner first.
- A web page instead of JSON, a "verify you are human" page, a
  403. That is a check for a person. Do not try to get past it,
  and do not retry in a loop. Take section 10.
- A 5xx, or no answer. Wait a minute and try once more. Then stop
  and tell the owner.

### 10. The other route -- the web form, with the owner at the screen

The upload page a person uses: `https://datatracker.ietf.org/submit/`

The cover sheet records a revision uploaded by the owner himself,
and he recalls having a browser agent do it while he watched.

The form takes the XML alone, under "XML source of the I-D"; the
text is made from it on the server. After the upload, the status
page shows the checks and what was read from the file, and the
same confirmation mail goes out.

It needs a browser, and it may put a check for a person in front
of the agent. So it is done with the owner watching. A browser
agent makes the motions. Anything that asks for a person -- a
human check, a sign-in, an agreement -- he does himself. The agent
stops and waits at each of those.

The same re-dated XML is used. Sections 5, 6, 8 and 11 hold
unchanged.

### 11. After both are posted -- the shelf

These are Muse's rules. They are listed so that nothing is missed.
Muse's records govern, not this list.

- Pull the posted -04 files from the IETF's own archive and
  compare them with the local renders. The server made its text
  from the XML with its own xml2rfc. The mirror rule wants the
  shelf to match the IETF text with no character different, so
  where they differ, the IETF's bytes are the ones to keep.
- Freeze the posted -04 set into each draft's `submitted/` folder.
- Update the cover sheet, `docs/ietf/status.md`: two new rows in
  the posted table, the submission numbers, the day of posting,
  the new expiry, and that the datatracker now expects -05.
- Update each draft's own `status.md`, and the "LOCAL DRAFT --
  UNSUBMITTED" header at the top of each -04 .md.
- The README's two datatracker links name no revision. They need
  no change.
- Run the reader-pages test and the other docs guards, then the
  release, as usual.
- Nothing in this job asks for mail to the ISE. Any word to the
  editor is the owner's to send or to order.

### 12. What the owner is told, and how

Short lines, with times in Pacific. Five moments are worth one
line each:

1. Both drafts are re-dated and re-rendered, and only the dates
   moved.
2. Version 1 is uploaded, the IETF's mail is in his inbox under
   such a subject, and he opens it and presses confirm.
3. Version 1 is posted at -04.
4. The same two for version 2.
5. The shelf is updated, and both drafts now expire on such a day.

### 13. What I checked, and what I did not

Read on 15-Nov, first hand:

- The repository at main, 46dc42f2f: both -04 folders, the dates
  in the two -04 .md files and the two -04 XML files, the dates in
  all eight posted XML files, the idnits records, the cover sheet
  and the -04 applied record.
- xmllint on both -04 XML files: well-formed.
- The API's page: the fields, the form type, the answers, the
  status call, the confirmation by email.
- The IETF 127 dates page: the cut-off on Monday 2-Nov, the
  meeting from 14-Nov in San Francisco.
- The upload page: no closure notice.
- Version 2's datatracker page: latest revision 03.

From the datatracker's source code as I remember it, not opened
that evening:

- The exact words of the date rule, and its 3 days.
- The state names "auth", "aut-appr" and "posted". The API's page
  itself names only "validating" and "cancel", and shows "auth"
  in its example.
- Who the confirmation mail goes to for a new revision.
- The exact moment the window reopened.
- The words of the errors in section 9.

Not checked at all: whether the owner has a datatracker login, and
version 1's datatracker page.

I sent nothing to the datatracker; I only read those pages. This
entry is the one change in the repository.

