## 12-Nov-9:10pm-PST — 3.16.25.1 on a green main; the docs group stops cancelling; a release's day is the UTC day

The registry line landed at 04:46Z, the Linux suite on that head
read green in 9.3 minutes with both Windows parts, and 3.16.25.1
was prepared on a main the train read green: release at 5.3
minutes, every mirror by 7.2. Four things for every seat.

The version's third figure is 25. The tree measured 24,999,648
tokens at 3.16.24.22 and 25,004,714 at this cut. The fold of the
written-out lists (Q172) takes the measure back to about 24.3
million and the figure holds at 25 by version4's odometer rule;
Q172 carries the addendum, so S3's "stays 24" reads "holds at
25".

Only the rolling group cancels a running build now. The docs
group is named by the head commit's message, so a push whose head
is a desk note over code builds its suites in that group, where
the next desk note or web edit cancelled them: the builds of
0b59937c and 644358bc lost their suites that way on 13 Nov, the
second with a new test module that has no hosted verdict yet,
and the build that carried the registry line was cancelled two
minutes after its suites had read. A desk note's build waits
behind a suite instead; nothing gates on it.

A release is dated by the UTC day, whichever machine cuts: the
train and the figures tool read the hosted record's clock, and
the changelog's entries for the releases of 13 Nov carry the
13th (they read 12, 13, 12 down the evening from two local
clocks).

`release_train.py status` prints "no legs on this head" for a
push of docs alone, where it printed "red" for the skipped legs;
"red" is kept for a leg that ran and failed. The line under it is
the one the loop's first step reads: main's newest suite verdict.

## 12-Nov-8:50pm-PST — a red suite on main is read where a release is prepared (Q174)

