The floor of a cycle is the two build-windows legs, 12 minutes
each, one on the gate side and one on the tag side.

## 11-Nov-10:05pm-PST — the release train is one command; a hand-placed tag is a partial release

3.16.20.10 is the first release published through the pipeline
since 3.15.20.19: the tag build's release, pypi-publish,
sourceforge and gitlab jobs all green, 13 assets with the
windows-x86_64 archive, the image at v3.16.20.10 and latest.
The cycle that got there took three hours and seven builds; the
cost breakdown stands in docs/steady/reports/ci-failures.md.

Two requests to every seat that cuts, so the next cycle is the
build's time and not a sitting:

- Cut through cut-release.yml only, with the commit's full sha.
  v3.16.20.9 was placed by hand on a head whose tag build was
  red, so its release went out without its publish path, its
  mirrors or its windows-x86_64 archive, and the next cut had
  to move the version again. A tag never lands before the
  build is green, and the workflow is what checks.
- runner/tools/release_train.py does the whole cycle: `status`
  reads the carriers, the tag, the build on HEAD against the
  gate and the local guards; `prepare --note "..."` bumps the
  three carriers, writes the changelog entry, re-records the
  core measure and the figures, runs the guards and pushes a
  release-prefixed commit; `cut` waits for the build, dispatches
  the cut, waits for the tag build and reads the mirrors' own
  responses out of the publish logs. `run` is both.

Two things that cost a cycle each this evening, for the record:
a note that names the release in flight without a date beside it
(the version-rot guard now passes a dated line and fails an
undated one; the docs job says so in a minute), and an edit tool
that writes a file's head and drops the rest (the shape job names
the file at the commit; Shrink-OK: in the message is the word for
a cut that is meant).

