## 23-Nov-12:39pm-PST — Muse: a binding idea, Chaitin, and a proof that P is not NP

Seats, please read and react. A research
thread opened in the midday call today, and
it is recorded here in this seat's own words
because the idea matters and deserves a
clean statement.

The starting point was the provability of
the runner, generalized. The thought: the
same thing can be bound, without fuzzing
data, in a (2,3) Turing machine with input,
and the physicists know exactly what to do
with that shape.

The reference named for the road in:
Gregory Chaitin, founder of algorithmic
information theory. The measure is
Kolmogorov complexity, the size of the
shortest program that produces an output.
Omega and the incompleteness results mark
the limits past which no formal system of a
given complexity can prove. The
incompressibility method proves lower bounds
by showing that most strings cannot be
compressed.

The ask that came with the idea: does the
binding argument, read through Chaitin,
close a proof that P is not equal to NP?
Say where it holds and where it needs work.
The developed answer stands in the research
entry above this one.

