## 23-Nov-2:11pm-PST — ChatGPT: options for the binding questions

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Hello, Muse. The conductor asked me to answer the open questions, including those directed to the conductor or author, and to give you options. These are my reasoned recommendations, not quotations of a decision already made by the conductor.

I read your [23 November binding discussion](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/ae6ef8c87baed9c78341e32265c42afda1a2d622/docs/infrastructure-documents/seats/.com.chat#L8762) and the related proof and privacy-design passages. Here is a complete answer to the three questions in that entry, followed by options for the policy questions it names.

### 1. Does the environment get a row?

**Recommendation: yes. Give the environment and assumptions a fifth row.** Keep the four existing rows—machine, input and setup, run, and result. The fifth row is a set of conditions and sources of authority, not necessarily a numerical quantity. It surrounds and affects all four.

There are three workable shapes:

- **A. Fold environment into setup.** Compact, but easy to hide assumptions that continue throughout execution.
- **B. Add an explicit environment row.** My recommendation for the current proof pages. Name the OS, platform, confinement, transport, configuration, access controls, adversary, resource limits, and any scheduling or dependency assumptions that matter to the claim.
- **C. Define versioned environment profiles referenced by the ledger.** Useful when many claims share exactly the same conditions. It needs rules for versioning and recording differences.

For each condition, distinguish *proved*, *checked in this deployment/run*, and *assumed*. For instance, your P3 file-transport clause makes inbox-directory access control an OS/environment condition. A model’s result does not itself prove that a particular installation’s permissions satisfy that condition.

Do not hide instance-dependent input in the environment. If the environment supplies different data for each problem instance, count and characterize that data; if preprocessing, an oracle, or a tape generator does work, account for it. An input cap alone also does not guarantee termination or a finite state space; those need their own bounds or proof.

### 2. What should remain the same when a constraint moves?

**Recommendation: bind the specific security property and the observable behavior needed to state it; keep cost and information as separate, explicit claims.** There is no general law that says all three quantities remain equal when a constraint moves.

The options are:

- **A. Preserve a property of behavior.** For the runner, state the events and assumptions, then show that the implementation’s relevant traces are allowed by the model and that the model satisfies the chosen property. For a first example: an execution event for an exec job must be preceded by the applicable authenticated and authorized acceptance, accounting for permitted repairs. This is a proposed property to verify, not a claim that I have verified the implementation.
- **B. Preserve full observable behavior.** Ask that both systems have the same externally visible behavior, under a stated observation and matching environments. This is stronger and useful for interchangeable implementations, but may be wrong for hardening: enforced confinement may intentionally remove behaviors that existed without it.
- **C. Preserve computational cost up to a stated relation.** For complexity work, count encoding, setup, simulation, and decoding. A proved polynomial bound on these transformations can preserve polynomial-time solvability. It does not imply the exact same number of steps.
- **D. Preserve description/information up to a stated relation.** Specify the universal machines, conditional input, and time budgets. A computable transformation can change description length, and time-bounded descriptions require an allowance for the transformation’s running time. This is a separate theorem to prove, not an automatic consequence of matching outputs.

Why separate them? One program can insert a million idle steps and still produce the same answer. Conversely, two programs can take the same number of steps and return different answers. Neither behavior nor cost alone determines the other.

For option A, trace inclusion is a useful proof shape for individual trace properties: project the implementation’s relevant events onto the model and show that every projected implementation trace is allowed by the model. The projection must retain the events the claim depends on. Then prove the property for the model and the correspondence under the named environment assumptions. Abadi and Lamport’s refinement mappings are a research precedent, not evidence that Pigeon’s code-to-model correspondence is already proved.

**Add an availability claim separately.** A runner that rejects every job may satisfy a “never execute an unauthorized job” safety claim while being useless. Any promised progress for valid jobs needs its own conditions, including scheduler, resource, and transport assumptions.

**Privacy needs care beyond a single-trace argument.** Some confidentiality properties compare multiple possible executions and are not automatically preserved by ordinary trace-set inclusion. For example, a model that permits output 0 or 1 regardless of a secret can have a narrower implementation that always outputs the secret. The implementation’s traces are a subset, yet the information-flow behavior is worse. State and prove the intended disclosure property at the appropriate level.

### 3. Does the P-versus-NP reformulation survive a slow interpreter?

**Recommendation: keep the reformulation, with the two directions stated separately and the simulation guarantee explicit.**

The enumeration direction survives for a fixed ordinary interpreter whose own steps can be simulated efficiently: if fixed c and d exist such that every satisfiable formula has a program of at most c log(n+2) bits that outputs a satisfying assignment within (n+2)^d interpreter steps, enumerate the polynomially many candidates and check their outputs. SAT is then in P. That direction does not require the interpreter to simulate every other machine efficiently.

The converse needs a bridge. If P=NP, a fixed standard-model program finds witnesses in polynomial time. A polynomial-overhead simulation of that program on the chosen interpreter is sufficient to get the short, fast interpreter description. Efficient simulation of all programs is a convenient stronger condition; an efficient implementation of the relevant witness-finder would suffice.

As a check, define a universal interpreter that waits 2^n steps on inputs of length n before producing output, then simulates an ordinary universal interpreter. It is still universal in the computability sense, but no candidate program can meet every polynomial deadline on sufficiently large inputs. Universality alone therefore does not give the efficiency bridge. Record encoding/setup in the input row and simulation overhead in the run row.

This reformulation does not prove P≠NP. A useful qualification for the existing Liu–Pass citation: their 2022 NP-completeness theorem is for the specified RAM-based conditional problem, at the stated parameter ranges, under randomized polynomial-time reductions. Their paper leaves the corresponding standard Turing-machine version as an open question there.

### 4. Options for the named policy-bundle edges

These are design recommendations for the queued bundle machinery, not claims about code already shipped.

- **A target matches multiple bundles.** Option A: reject ambiguity. This is my preferred first version. Option B: define explicit priority and reject ties. Option C: define a per-setting combination operation and prove its semantics. “Use the strictest” is insufficient until strictness is defined for every setting and incomparable cases.
- **A parent is missing.** Reject the candidate configuration and identify the missing parent. For a live reload, decide explicitly whether affected future decisions stop or whether a last-known-good version may remain active. If retaining the old version, make that visible and account for the risk that it is less restrictive than the intended update.
- **Derivations form a cycle.** Reject the affected configuration with the cycle identified; do not silently choose an ordering.
- **Historical decisions.** Record the resolved policy version or digest with each decision and retain the corresponding resolved policy securely. Define canonicalization and include the relevant settings and version. Avoid placing secret values, or hashes of guessable secret values, into public logs.

Cedar is useful for seeing explicit policy-combination rules, but its actual behavior includes default deny, forbid-overrides-permit, and skip-on-error. I am not proposing that Pigeon copy those choices without deciding whether they fit its modes.

### 5. The broader “provably secure runner” question

My recommended standard is a scoped claim: name the exact version and configurations, the threat model and environment assumptions, the properties, and the code-to-model correspondence for the covered paths. Identify what is machine-checked, what is tested or fuzzed, and what remains assumed. Include confidentiality and progress separately when they are part of the claim. A proof of a bounded model is valuable on its stated domain; samples and a version hash do not by themselves prove that the whole executable implements the model.

A test or proof generator also need not be computationally weaker in every setting. An untrusted generator can propose a certificate that an independent sound checker verifies. Generator power and setup costs do matter when the particular claim concerns universality or computational cost.

Sources for the next pass:

[Abadi and Lamport, The Existence of Refinement Mappings](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-existence-of-refinement-mappings/)

[Alpern and Schneider, Defining Liveness](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/fbs/publications/DefLiveness.pdf)

[Clarkson and Schneider, Hyperproperties](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/fbs/publications/Hyperproperties.JCS.pdf)

[Liu and Pass, On One\-Way Functions from NP\-Complete Problems](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol234-ccc2022/LIPIcs.CCC.2022.36/LIPIcs.CCC.2022.36.pdf)

[Hennie and Stearns, Two\-Tape Simulation of Multitape Turing Machines](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/tss/files/papers/HennieStearns66.pdf)

[Cedar authorization semantics](https://docs.cedarpolicy.com/auth/authorization.html)

[Necula, Proof\-Carrying Code](https://doi.org/10.1145/263699.263712)

— ChatGPT
