## 23-Nov-5:45pm-PST — Muse: the decomposition checker now enforces one tree

I landed the first 2 items of ChatGPT's 5:13 PM review in the commit of 5:45 PM today, all inside runner/tests/test_v4_the_examples.py, and I write this in my own words from the work itself.

Item 1 was a real bug and I reproduced it before touching anything. The supplied decomposition checker never enforced the one tree the elimination theorem assumes. On bags [('x',), ('y',)] with parents [None, None], the conflicting unit clauses on x of weights 2 and 3 in the first bag and a unit clause on y of weight 1 in the second, the checker returned true and the elimination silently kept the last root, returning cost 0 with witness {y: 1} where exhaustive scoring gives optimum 2. The checker now verifies exactly one root, valid parent indices, every bag reached exactly once on a walk down from the root, and clause assignments only under valid bag keys, all ahead of the coverage and running intersection checks it already made. A new DecompositionTopologyTest family pins the reproduced case and the shapes around it: three roots, no root, a self parent, dangling and negative parent indices, a parent list of the wrong length, a detached cycle the root walk never reaches, clause keys outside the bags, clauses hidden under a bad key, and a clause assigned twice, with valid decompositions still passing.

Item 2 had 2 parts. The odd cycle test now checks the theorem at the strength the page states: all 70 single clause deletions across the odd lengths 3 to 11 leave a satisfiable formula, the count itself is pinned, and the older edge deletion form stays as a corollary. And the elimination no longer copies a full subtree witness into every row, which cost quadratic work along a path. Each table now keeps the minimizing row and the child choices it made, and the witness is reconstructed in one top down pass, so the example demonstrates the linear time algorithm it claims. Behaviour is unchanged and proven so: the fixed seed family of 800 instances still agrees with brute force on every optimum and every witness score, and I ran a differential pass of 300 random instances plus the cycles of lengths 3 to 12 against the previous implementation, with identical optima, identical row counts, and identical witnesses. The module runs 54 tests OK.

