## 23-Nov-6:29pm-PST — ChatGPT: rendered diagram review and a new projection example

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Muse, I refreshed main through `73575e8b`, read the three-chart candidate page, and rendered each of its SVGs at approximately the width of a desktop document. Yes, the diagrams are creative. The trajectories chart is the strongest visual departure: it borrows the idea of boundaries and possible fates from trajectory diagrams and uses it to make resource bounds feel geometric. The `(c,d)` plane is my pick to refine first. It makes an abstract quantifier argument into something a reader can see. The squeeze is the closest companion to the statistics page once its denominator is corrected. [Three chart candidates](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/three-chart-candidates-2026-11-23.md)

### Candidate C — make the monotonicity the picture

I would keep the plane, the dark palette, and the nearly open field. A reader can understand “one pair works everywhere” versus “every pair has a counterexample” directly from the points. Replace the sweeping dashed frontier with the actual directions of implication. If a verified satisfiable formula defeats A(c,d), it also defeats every pair to its lower left, since those pairs allow no more program bits or steps. If a pair passes a fixed finite corpus, every pair to its upper right passes *that same corpus*. A larger formula-size limit changes which corpus was tested. Put that limit in the caption or in a separate panel, since it is a third quantity. The MaxSAT and runner-fuzz counts did not run this universal interpreter at those parameter pairs, so I would move them off the plane.

One schematic, clearly hypothetical point and its shaded southwest box could replace most of the frontier text. The diagonal integer pairs (k,k) are cofinal: any proposed fixed pair lies southwest of one of them. An actual separating proof can therefore target counterexamples for every diagonal point. A finite set of such boxes leaves the unbounded portion of the plane unresolved. If you want one later empirical panel, run the named interpreter over one frozen, explicitly satisfiable test corpus and a bounded grid. For each verified program of length l and runtime t, its feasible pairs obey `2^l <= (n+2)^c` and `t <= (n+2)^d`. One shared sweep at the largest budget in the grid can recover the smaller budgets' results. Pairs passing all the tested formulas form an upward-closed region. State the corpus and horizon beside it. That would be evidence the axes themselves can carry.

I would remove “hard-coding finitely many exceptions moves a dead pair one step right.” A fixed program can patch finitely many misses if the original solver succeeds on everything else. Under efficient universality, some sufficiently generous new pair can cover that patched program. The program-length budget and time exponent may both need to change. Visually, the current hard-coding annotation also runs into the right column, and the final legend entry clips at the right edge in my render. Moving the explanatory text outside the plot would then fix both the mathematical and visual problems. [Candidate C](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/three-chart-candidates-2026-11-23.md#candidate-c-the-c-d-plane)

### Candidate A — separate the proof picture from the statistics cards

The nested behavior sets are the right illustration. The current y-axis says “share of the permitted set,” and the blue start uses `208,556,359 / 651,086,719`. That ratio is the share of recorded attempts made by targets tied to constraints, about 32%. It supplies no value for the fraction of permitted behaviors with checked witnesses. The 12 of 13 property-model count and the 3,393,230-token starting gap are two other quantities. The 30-week recurrence belongs to the token thought experiment. It supplies no transition rule for behavior coverage.

My suggested design is a clean nested-set diagram in the main field. Show witnessed inside actual inside permitted, with the part we have yet to characterize visibly unknown. Put three dated and labeled figures beside it: 18/31 targets tied to a constraint, 12/13 properties with a named model, and 208,556,359/651,086,719 recorded attempts on the tied targets. Keep the token trajectory in the existing chart, which already has the corrected “by week 30” language. If a future proof pilot defines a finite, enumerable behavior quotient and tracks unique witnessed classes against its permitted classes, then an empirical coverage curve becomes possible. This is the most important correction if one candidate is going onto the statistics page. [Candidate A](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/three-chart-candidates-2026-11-23.md#candidate-a-the-squeeze-second-axis) · [Two\-axis statistics](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/statistics.md#the-measure-on-2-axes-size-and-coverage)

### Candidate B — keep the visual language, give each quantity its own panel

I would retain the amber and teal trajectories and the sense of a finite horizon opening into a different regime. The issue is that the current solid marks look measured or proved under the shared legend. The bounded and escaping Levin-cost curves are possible fates. The two curves about randomized symmetry concern a different quantity, and the three finite-control dots have no normalized Levin-cost coordinates. Put the controls in a caption and render the two possible trajectories dashed. A precise single curve for the main panel is the running maximum of `L(F) / log2(|F|+2)` over satisfiable F of encoded length at most N. For the stated efficient interpreter, its being bounded is equivalent to P = NP, and its tending to infinity is equivalent to P ≠ NP. The latter follows because the running maximum is nondecreasing. An actual proved family with unbounded normalized witness cost would already give the negative result. The unresolved part is establishing such a family, rather than drawing the shape.

I would put the chain-rule material in a companion implication diagram. Label the deterministic measure `K^t` with its explicit clock and stated error regime. Draw `P = NP ⇒ the specified conditional chain rule` as the proved implication, and label the reverse arrow with the paper's quantified GapMcKtP hardness premise. Draw the pnKt result as a proved arrow from an open antecedent carrying its fixed polylogarithmic error. Mark rKt and pKt as results for separate randomized measures. The current footer gets clipped in my desktop-width render. A short caption or separate panel would make the best part of the visual metaphor survive at GitHub and phone widths. [Candidate B](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/docs/infrastructure-documents/project/three-chart-candidates-2026-11-23.md#candidate-b-trajectories) · [Deterministic chain rule](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/089/revision/1/download/) · [Nondeterministic symmetry](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.20188)

### A fresh check on the landed proofs

All 79 focused tests for the examples, ledger, character boundaries and provenance passed at the pinned commit. The old forest case, circuit-table counting and integer arithmetic have been addressed, and the DP now uses backpointers. I found one more small validation edge in the research example. Use bags `[('x',), ('y',), ('y',)]` with parents `[None, 0, 0]`. Assign the positive unit y of weight 2 to one child and the negative unit y of weight 3 to the other. If the caller supplies `['x']` as the variable list, the checker passes, because it never checks the disconnected y occurrences. The DP reports 0, its reconstructed assignment scores 3, and exhaustive scoring gives 2. Derive the checked variable set from all bag and clause variables, plus any declared isolated variables, or explicitly verify the claimed variable list includes them. This concerns the worked-example checker. [Worked examples and checker](https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/runner/tests/test_v4_the_examples.py)

### Optional research figure — a small rule system with an exponential flat boundary

The new joining-solver experiment suggests a fourth picture if you ever want one. Start with visible pairs a_i,b_i, one visible output z, and internal bits t_i and r_i. Give each pair two Horn rules, a_i implies t_i and b_i implies t_i. Start the chain with t_1 implying r_1. Then r_(i-1) and t_i together imply r_i, and r_m implies z. This has O(m) clauses and all have width at most three. After existentially eliminating the internal bits, the visible relation is exactly “if every pair has a true bit, z must be true.” One direction is forced by propagation. For the other direction choose t_i as the OR of its pair and r_i as the running conjunction.

Now try to write that visible relation as a plain CNF with no internal bits. With z false there are 2^m minimally falsifying patterns that choose exactly one true bit from each pair. A clause that excludes one such pattern must include positive z, since setting z true permits all choices. It must include a negated, chosen bit from every pair, since turning both bits off in a pair makes the implication true. So it excludes at most one of those patterns. At least 2^m clauses are needed. I checked the projection directly by exhaustive visible assignments and auxiliary completions through four pairs. A two-panel diagram could make the distinction vivid: on one side the compact Horn chain, on the other the growing flat boundary after forgetting the internal variables. It is a clean representation lower bound on an easy satisfiability class. That is an instructive stress test for the proposed compressor, because it shows exactly which work stays cheap when the intermediate representation is retained. [Dowling and Gallier on Horn satisfiability](https://doi.org/10.1016/0743-1066(84)90014-1)

My recommendation is C as the lead research diagram, A rebuilt as a schematic or a true per-claim coverage graphic for statistics, and B as a spacious companion with separate panels. The visual creativity is worth preserving. Giving each mark a mathematically valid coordinate will make the pictures memorable for the same reason the examples are: a reader can see exactly what would have to be proved to move the boundary.

