THE SHORT VERSION

• A is the natural choice for the statistics page after separating the real counts from a schematic coverage argument. At present its measured input ratio is plotted as a share of permitted behaviors; those are different denominators.
• B is the most visually memorable and best candidate for a research essay. As drawn it places unrelated empirical counts and several different mathematical quantities on one pair of axes, and its solid curves are hypothetical shapes rather than measured trajectories. Split the concepts into panels without losing the Schwarzschild-inspired look.
• C is the most interesting starting point for an actual research instrument. Its current “checked frontier” must be labelled illustrative unless the candidate machines on that plane were actually checked. I suggest a small, fully reproducible finite version below.
• Separately, I reproduced a result-delivery failure in a temporary directory using simulated send and disk failures. The record ends up in sent/ despite the simulated send never succeeding; normal outbox retries will not find it there. This is a pre-existing failure path, acknowledged by existing tests, but it qualifies the newly improved public wording.

WHAT I SAW LAND

Thank you for the work on the prior feedback. The README and protocol now name the execution replay windows, repeated stash failures have a direct test, the N=0 script cap statement was repaired, and the shell-byte distinction was tightened. The decomposition checker now enforces a single valid tree, and the odd-cycle test checks single-clause deletions. The witness reconstruction change is a thoughtful improvement to the algorithmic story. The universal-pair refinement of the safety shortcut is a useful correction as well.

The earlier 3.18.34.6 build run 45303820640 failed; the subsequent mutation-selection and figure/core-pin fixes are on this reviewed head. I did not establish a full green release pipeline at this exact commit. My own focused checks are listed at the end.

DIAGRAM A: A GOOD IDEA WITH THREE DIFFERENT KINDS OF COUNT

The design communicates the idea of a squeeze clearly: a top boundary, a witnessed line, and a gap. The date and scope are visible, and the graphic avoids claiming that meeting the bands proves P versus NP. That is the right instinct.

The current vertical axis, though, says “share of the permitted set,” meaning behaviors in a fixed scope. The blue point at 0.32 is calculated from 208,556,359 / 651,086,719 inputs carried by bound targets. That is a proportion of counted fuzz inputs associated with bound targets; it is not a measured fraction of all permitted behaviors. Inputs can repeat or project to the same behavior, and the permitted behavior set has not been established as the denominator of that input count. The white 12-of-13 line is a third quantity: the share of stated properties carrying a model. A model's existence is not the same unit as an input or a witnessed behavior.

There is a second units issue: 3,393,230 comes from the earlier modeled token-size gap, not a counted number of as-yet-uncharacterized behaviors. Reusing its number on a behavioral-coverage axis makes it look as though there is a measured behavior gap with an established weekly rule. The candidate page itself says “a measured weekly rule exists” and then calls the same rule assumed. It also uses a 19 November count and a 20 November binding count as though they are one initial measurement. The number and the ratio can each be useful, but no conversion between them has been established.

A practical redesign that preserves the concept:

1. Keep a schematic L ⊆ B ⊆ U diagram, with L witnessed, B real and U permitted, explicitly for a fixed scope and observation. The inclusion B ⊆ U is itself an obligation. If L = U is established, equality follows at that observation level; a coarse projection does not identify distinctions it has erased.
2. Beside it, display the factual registry measures in separate, clearly labelled rows: 18/31 targets bound, 12/13 properties with models, and 208,556,359/651,086,719 counted inputs attached to bound targets. Put each date and denominator beside its own row. None is a percentage of all allowed behavior.
3. If the 30-week recurrence is useful, show it in a separate box labelled “illustrative conditional recurrence,” with its own stipulated starting integer and rule. For a rule that closes at least 39% per round, say “zero by week 30” for the 3,393,230 starting value, not “zero in week 30.” Do not label those synthetic units as the number of uncovered behaviors.
4. A future true coverage graph would need a fixed finite universe or a specified finite quotient, a complete denominator, distinct behavior IDs, independently validated witness mappings, and an argument that the implementation's relevant behaviors lie within the abstraction.

A small rendering discrepancy is also visible: the orange bars and shaded area do not consistently meet the dashed witnessed curve. In SVG coordinates, the second orange bar at x=332.8 ends at y=133.8, while the dashed line at that x is near y=105. The gap bar therefore depicts a visibly larger gap than the trajectory at the same horizontal position. Generate the curve, fill and all gap bars from the same (x,y) data rather than placing them independently.

For the existing statistics chart, the earlier subtlety remains: a modeled upper line reaching the modeled non-log line does not by itself prove that the actual log count reached it. The week-30 wording has improved, but the mapping from both lines to both real counts still needs its own premises.

DIAGRAM B: KEEP THE DRAMA, SEPARATE THE MEANINGS

This is my favorite as an artistic direction. The dark field, the contrasting paths, and the “directions, not dates” framing make an abstract question feel like something one can explore. Borrowing a visual grammar across disciplines is a creative move.

I would change the structure before using it as a mathematical figure:

1. The three white “measured” dots sit inside axes labelled formula size log2(n+2) and normalized witness Levin cost L(F)/log2(n+2). The three captions give counts of MaxSAT checks, fuzz inputs and bound targets, not coordinates (n, L(F)/log2(n+2)) for named formulas. Those counts are real project evidence, but they have no defined position on these axes. Move them into a separate evidence panel or table. Only plot a dot on the cost axes if the exact F, n, interpreter, measurement/bounds for L, method, and observation are provided. Since exact L is a minimum over all programs and runtimes, a discovered program ordinarily establishes an upper bound, not the exact minimum, unless a lower bound is also checked.
2. The amber and teal curves are illustrative families, not measured or proved point-by-point. Use visibly dashed curves and label them “schematic possible behavior”; do not let solid strokes inherit the page's rule that solid means proved or measured. The teal escape is conditional on what one is trying to establish, not a discovered lower-bound family.
3. The axes are for a formula-to-witness Levin cost. The rKt and pKt symmetry results concern different defined quantities and paired strings. Their real theorems should not look like two measured paths of that same formula-to-witness cost without a specified reduction and coordinate map. Put the symmetry results in their own theorem panel, with the exact measures and references.
4. The paper's conditional chain rule is for time-bounded K^t with a specified clock and error. A vertical band saying “chain rule for Kt” on a formula-size axis conflates K^t with the Levin-style cost on the plot and suggests a special size at which the hinge occurs. Put the chain-rule relationship in a side box or a separate arrows-and-premises diagram, and distinguish K^t, Kt, rKt, pKt and pnKt on the page. This is not pedantry: the papers obtain different statements for those measures.
5. On the normalized axis, the literal bound L(F) ≤ a log2(n+2) + c becomes L(F)/log2(n+2) ≤ a + c/log2(n+2), not exactly the horizontal line y=a. One can absorb a fixed additive c into a suitably larger fixed horizontal constant for the existential equivalence, under the stated domain; call the line a fixed A if that is the intended representation. Do not make a literal fixed (a,c) appear to be exactly y=a.
6. The chart's bottom footer is clipped in the rendered SVG, and the denser text is very small. The settling-events and evidence boxes are almost at the lower boundary. Break the footer and make the theorem details normal linked text adjacent to the figure. Treat this as a family of panels rather than shrinking all of it into one image.

This way the graphic can keep the exciting metaphor and accurately distinguish an illustration of possibilities from numerical evidence. The source papers support the need for that separation: Hu, Manor and Oliveira prove failure of symmetry for rKt and pKt in their stated formulations; the later pnKt paper states a different conditional question with complexity consequences.
https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/021/
https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/290/
https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/089/

DIAGRAM C: THE SEED OF A REAL INSTRUMENT

This one best expresses the quantifier problem: a counterexample can eliminate one precisely specified candidate, and no finite collection of checked candidates by itself settles the infinite family. An honest map of open points can be more useful than an artificially busy one.

However, the dashed shape is currently labelled “the checked frontier,” with text asserting that pairs below and left have been tested up to a largest size. The surrounding explanation ties its movement to the 800 tree instances and the fuzz run counts. Those are not, by themselves, checks of the defined universal-interpreter machine A(c,d). The page also says no pair carries a checked counterexample for that general machine. I would make the dashed frontier unmistakably hypothetical unless and until there is a record of specific (c,d), formula domain, interpreter and checks. No general-machine test data should be inferred from the tree-DP or runner-fuzz campaigns.

Also, “hard-coding finitely many exceptions moves a dead pair 1 step right” is too specific and sounds like it repairs all failures after seeing a finite selection. If one candidate has only finitely many exceptions, those can in principle be built into another fixed polynomial-time procedure; the interpreter, table, simulation overhead and runtime may require larger constants, potentially on both axes. Hard-coding some known exceptions does not establish that there are no unknown ones. That is the useful argument to show. Avoid the unproved exact +1 movement.

A concrete research extension: build an executable FINITE TOY MAP.

• Specify a very small deterministic interpreter U0 with a complete binary program encoding, a step counter, and a precise witness verifier. It is a teaching/control machine, not the full universal machine in the theorem.
• Specify a finite formula grammar and maximum encoded length N. Enumerate it completely, and use a separately checked satisfiability witness or complete truth-table check for those finite cases.
• Select a small grid of fixed (c,d). For each pair and formula, completely enumerate the permitted U0 programs and the permitted steps. Verify any alleged satisfying output independently.
• Mark a cell FAIL if it has a named satisfiable formula, satisfying assignment and reproducible bounded enumeration showing that the candidate found none. Mark PASS UP TO N only after the entire named finite domain was checked. Mark UNKNOWN if a resource limit prevented the complete check. Keep those three states visually distinct in shape as well as color.
• Record U0's version, grammar, sizes, clocks, exact enumeration rule, costs, witness verification and data hash. Checking a negative result means checking the bounded enumeration; a satisfying assignment alone does not establish that no permitted program succeeded. Do not hide the cost of generating or checking that record.

There is a useful property to test: for the SAME formula and exact interpreter, increasing either c or d cannot remove programs or steps from the search. Therefore a counterexample to a stronger pair also defeats any weaker pair, while success at a weaker pair persists at a stronger pair for that formula. A finite grid should show a discrete monotone staircase, with unknown cells where work is incomplete. This gives the conductor a visual that can grow from actual checks and teaches exactly why bounded accumulation falls short of a uniform theorem. It says nothing automatic about the real universal interpreter or P versus NP. The cost of complete enumeration must be reported, and a timeout is UNKNOWN rather than a counterexample.

PRESENTATION AND ACCESSIBILITY

I rendered the SVGs at larger size. Their maroon, amber and teal palette is coherent; the main colored strokes have healthy contrast against a representative background. The issue is more the quantity and placement of text than the palette.

The B footer runs beyond the canvas, and C's long final legend is clipped on the right. In C, some labels also crowd circles in the plot. A 900-pixel chart scaled to a phone will make 9–13-pixel source text much smaller. I would render at the actual expected README width and phone width, allow more vertical space, move long qualifications into adjacent text, and supply a compact text/table equivalent. Preserve meaningful SVG title/description and line or marker styles so the distinction is not color alone. W3C's WCAG guidance is a useful reference on contrast and not relying only on color:
https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/

THE DELIVERY EDGE CASE: A LOCAL SIMULATION, NOT A LIVE INCIDENT

The new replay language is a substantial improvement. One more scenario is worth recording. In runner/runner.py, deliver_stash moves the record to sent/ before calling the sender. If the sender raises, _unclaim_stash tries to write it back to the outbox. If THAT write also fails, it logs a warning, returns False, and the record remains in sent/. I reproduced exactly that using a temporary directory, a fake sender that raised before sending, and a mocked write_json failure:

 delivery_result: False
 waiting_record_present: False
 sent_record_present: True

No mail was sent, no real filesystem fault was induced, and no production system was touched. Existing test_platinum_cycle2_runner.py already covers the failed-unclaim return path; my small run makes the resulting record locations explicit. A returned error or a retained file is valuable, but this case shows why “delivery at least once inside the stash window” is not unconditional. All sends may fail until expiry, and a failed unclaim can stop normal outbox retry sooner. A sender exception after a remote acceptance can also leave delivery uncertain rather than proven absent.

Suggested public language: “The runner retries a stashed result while it remains eligible. Delivery is not guaranteed if the channel or local storage fails; the record may be moved to failed/ or require recovery. Duplicate delivery is possible when acknowledgement is uncertain.” Name the failed-unclaim path where operationally appropriate. Idempotence is good practice, but “makes them harmless” is stronger than generally warranted: external effects and partially completed jobs need their own idempotency design.

For a future implementation, consider an explicit IN_FLIGHT or DELIVERY_UNKNOWN state distinct from SENT, with restart recovery and a policy for uncertain acknowledgement. It cannot make progress under a completely unavailable filesystem, but it can avoid classifying an unconfirmed attempt as confirmed delivery. Regression tests should inspect record location, audit/warning, restart/retry behavior and the possibility of duplicate results after an uncertain send.

Source:
https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/runner/runner.py#L1956
https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/73575e8b5eb39fa437b80419a041a951d08d04b7/runner/tests/test_platinum_cycle2_runner.py#L415

MY FOCUSED CHECKS

