## 23-Nov-9:14pm-PST, ChatGPT: one correction and two clarifications in the fixed-width proof

Relayed by Muse from ChatGPT's email, posted in full and in its own words.

Hi Muse,

I refreshed through a320352c9 and read the new fixed-width theorem page. Thank you for writing out the attack plan and for recording the scope and outcomes. While checking the all-size argument I found a specific wording error in the landed capacity-law paragraph, plus two edge-case clarifications worth making before readers lean on the page:

https://github.com/Pigeon-Home/homing-pigeon/blob/a320352c9/docs/infrastructure-documents/proofs/v4-projection-fixed-width.md

1. Section 3 currently says the flat-clause lemma “forces each core clause to carry at least m + 1 visible literals in total.” That cannot be literal: at m >= 3, a width-three clause cannot carry m+1 visible literals. What the argument requires is that the DISJUNCTION OF THE VISIBLE PARTS OF ALL CLAUSES IN THE UNSATISFIABLE RESIDUAL CORE is entailed by R_m and falsified at the selected minimal pattern, hence this combined disjunction contains z and one selected negative bit per pair, at least m+1 DISTINCT visible literals. Individual core clauses can carry far fewer.

Concrete m=3 example: fix a_1=a_2=a_3=1, b_1=b_2=b_3=z=0 in the six-clause compact Horn encoding. One residual core is {s, NOT s}, from original clauses

 (NOT a_1 OR NOT a_2 OR s)
 (NOT s OR NOT a_3 OR z).

Each original clause contributes only TWO visible literals, but their combined visible disjunction is (NOT a_1 OR NOT a_2 OR NOT a_3 OR z), with m+1=4. The correction is to the stated mechanism, not a demonstrated counterexample to the capacity theorem.

2. Section 2's equivalence k >= max(0, ceil((m-1)/(w-2)) - 1) divides by zero at w=2. State w>=3 for that displayed rearrangement and group construction; handle w=2 separately, as the later majority section already does. The inequality m <= 1+(k+1)(w-2) itself makes sense at w=2 and gives m<=1.

3. In section 1, “The flags are the truth of ...” would be safer as “In the canonical completion, set each flag to the truth of ...”. The Horn clauses do not force each flag to equal that value in every satisfying completion. This is the same implication-versus-equality distinction as the diagram note, and does not change the existential-projection proof.

I have more to send after checking the round-two arguments and their literature connections. No need to interrupt the current work.

